## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical use of for_each with maps, resource interdependencies, and stable identities across environments. Delivers hands-on pattern candidates expect in production.

## Key Concepts
- for_each with maps provides stable keys and predictable IDs
- referencing attributes from one resource in another (subnet_id in NAT GW)
- tagging and AZ assignment per subnet
- outputs to surface IDs for downstream resources
- when to prefer for_each vs count (stable keys vs positional indexing)

## Code Example
```javascript
variable "subnets" {
  type = map(object({ cidr_block = string; az = string }))
  default = {
    az1 = { cidr_block = "10.0.1.0/24", az = "us-east-1a" },
    az2 = { cidr_block = "10.0.2.0/24", az = "us-east-1b" },
    az3 = { cidr_block = "10.0.3.0/24", az = "us-east-1c" }
  }
}

resource "aws_subnet" "private" {
  for_each = var.subnets
  vpc_id            = var.vpc_id
  cidr_block        = each.value.cidr_block
  availability_zone = each.value.az
  tags = {
    Name = "private-${each.key}"
  }
}

output "private_subnet_ids" {
  value = [for s in aws_subnet.private : s.id]
}
```

```javascript
resource "aws_nat_gateway" "ngw" {
  for_each = aws_subnet.private
  allocation_id = aws_eip.ngw[each.key].id
  subnet_id     = aws_subnet.private[each.key].id
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- When would you choose for_each over count in real projects?
- How would you migrate from count-based to for_each-based resources without breaking state?
- How would you validate AZ distribution and CIDR correctness after apply?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of cross-account dependency management, enforcement of security policies, and plan-driven gating in CI.

## Key Concepts
- Provider aliasing across accounts
- Cross-account data sources
- depends_on for orchestration
- Plan JSON validation
- CI gating for safety

## Code Example
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  alias = "sec"
  region = "us-east-1"
  profile = "security"
}

data "aws_kms_key" "policy_key" {
  provider = aws.sec
  key_id   = "alias/security/default"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "bucket" {
  # ...
  server_side_encryption_configuration {
    rule {
      apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
        sse_algorithm   = "aws:kms"
        kms_master_key_id = data.aws_kms_key.policy_key.key_id
      }
    }
  }
  depends_on = [data.aws_kms_key.policy_key]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle revoking access to the security key if a key rotation is required?
- How would you test this in a multi-region, multi-account rollout without impacting existing buckets?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests environment isolation via provider aliases and per-env backends, plus CI gating for safe promotion to QA.

## Key Concepts
- Provider alias isolation
- Backend per environment
- CI gated plan/apply workflow

## Code Example
```hcl
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.4"

  backend "s3" {
    key    = "envs/qa/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-west-2"
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  alias = "qa"
  region = "us-west-2"
  assume_role {
    role_arn = "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_QA:role/TerraformRole"
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle secrets across environments?
- What are trade-offs of provider alias vs separate workspaces?
